conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8409232Z ^[[32mWelcome to the Multi-User Nix Installation
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8421111Z ^[[0m
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8421907Z This installation tool will set up your computer with the Nix package
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8422532Z manager. This will happen in a few stages:
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8422885Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8423480Z 1. Make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix. If it does, I
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8424385Z    will show you instructions on how to clean up your old install.
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8424713Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8425038Z 2. Show you what I am going to install and where. Then I will ask
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8425824Z    if you are ready to continue.
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8426599Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8427520Z 3. Create the system users ^[[34m(uids [30001..30008])^[[0m and groups ^[[34m(gid 30000)^[[0m
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8428624Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8429730Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8430405Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8430810Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8431180Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8431663Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8431962Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8432383Z    can use Nix.
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8432645Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8432772Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8432951Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8437199Z ^[[32m^[[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8438476Z ^[[0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8445655Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8445873Z I will:
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8446209Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8446452Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8447041Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8448118Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8448802Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8449138Z  - install Nix in /nix
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8449501Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8449939Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8450366Z    /root
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8463372Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8463988Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8475755Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8476325Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8476874Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8478547Z ^[[32m^[[4;32mReady to continue?
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8479247Z ^[[0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8498786Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8499383Z ^[[34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8510506Z ^[[0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8511397Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8511837Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8512044Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8512391Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8512723Z 
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8512939Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
conformance-update (ubuntu-latest)	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-11-06T04:25:45.8513365Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
